      "content_html": "The U.S. Senate on Tuesday voted 62-21 to advance a bipartisan bill to speed federal permits for energy and infrastructure projects, clearing a second procedural hurdle without passing the measure. The Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act would change approval rules for power lines, power plants, pipelines, mines and data centers; the Senate voted 73-25 to begin debate on Monday.\n\nSupporters say faster reviews could help energy projects and grid upgrades get built as electricity demand rises. The proposal would apply to renewable and fossil-fuel projects and require large data-center operators to pay more of the costs of grid infrastructure serving them.\n\nThe Senate is expected to consider amendments after Tuesday’s vote, with some Democrats seeking further changes to protections for tribal consultation and historic sites. If the Senate passes the bill, it would still need House approval; House Democratic Leader Hakeem Jeffries has said he wants more involvement from his caucus before the House acts.",
